myko caches one materialized map per distinct (query_id, params_hash) ‚Äî server/context.rs:298
builds the key as {host_id}:{kind}:{id}:{payload_hash}. Sharing across clients works
correctly, but a query parameterized by parent id gets a separate materialization per
distinct parameter value, and each one mirrors its ENTIRE source map (hyphae/lv-c682).

Measured on the rack deployment (2026-07-26, myko 4.0.0-canary.79, 8 clients):
  82,164 total records across 156 declared entity types (49 non-empty)
  top 10 types hold 96.8% of records; largest is Action @ 25,457
  data at rest ~40-170 MB; observed process RSS tens of GB

Per hyphae measurements, one materialized select over the Action map costs ~1 MB floor
regardless of how few rows match. A few thousand live materializations over mid-sized maps
reaches single-digit GB before accounting for plan depth ‚Äî each chained operator stage
carries its own MapState.

Not yet measured: how many materialized stages are actually live on a running server. That
is the multiplier the whole estimate rests on, and myko already has the accessors to answer
it ‚Äî query_cache_len / query_cache_live_count / view_cache_len / view_cache_live_count on
MykoServerContext. They are not currently surfaced through any report, so there is no way to
read them from a deployed node.

Suggested follow-ups:
  1. Expose cache sizes + live counts via a diagnostic report so a running server can be
     inspected (also answers whether dead weak-ref entries are accumulating vs being swept).
  2. Re-measure once hyphae/lv-c682 lands.
  3. If materialization count is the multiplier rather than per-stage cost, consider whether
     parameterized queries should share a single materialization over the source with
